**Read:** Revisit the sampling and measurement foundations, then [Industrial Water Treatment Operation and Maintenance — UFC 3-230-13](../sources/Industrial-Water-Treatment-UFC-3-230-13.lingua#r_41KNKBCA6GZWQ6VAJGAPG2HJ3R), chapter 7, and [Assessment of Treatment Plant Performance and Water Quality Data — von Sperling, Verbyla and Oliveira](../sources/Treatment-Plant-Performance-and-Water-Quality-Data.lingua#r_5PZG8VN422GHD657FBDR5KRWGS). Use the instrument methods and reference materials appropriate to the quantity being claimed.

An instrument value has a physical history: location, sampling line, transport time, conditioning, sensor response, calibration, conversion, timestamp and storage. A correct number can describe the wrong place or an earlier condition. A missing value, a value below a reporting limit and a real zero carry different information.

For an original transport example, a 12-metre sample line with 4-millimetre internal diameter contains about 0.151 litres. At 0.1 litre/minute its nominal displacement time is about 1.51 minutes, before sensor response or additional volumes. Real residence-time spreading, wall interactions and stopped flow can invalidate a simple delay estimate. An apparently rapid control action cannot make that measurement current.

**Work:** Trace one flow and one quality measurement from the physical process to a plotted value. State range, resolution, uncertainty, response, unit and time basis. Compare an independent observation with the online signal. Introduce a blocked line, drift and a clock mismatch in synthetic data; explain how each affects a balance and a decision. For advanced work, estimate a response model from a designed experiment and test it on a separate transient.

**Return:** [Water, sanitation and industrial utilities](../Water-sanitation-and-industrial-utilities.lingua#r_7B9GRCJFHVBEEQEXKWCR2QMMPN).
